*Problem and Contribution:*
Statistical tasks used to rely a lot on deterministic methods. Since the advent and
broad use of LLM-based tools, data imputation has been done using data agents.
However, the trust in these non-deterministic tools depends both on the results and
the underlying assumptions made by the data agent. This project compares a data agent
across a large number of datasets with values being MAR (missing at random),
MCAR (missing completely at random) or MNAR (missing not at random), respectively.
Much of the recent work in the field (David Donoho, 2017; Sun Ming et al., 2025)
evaluated systems based on task performance, often overlooking whether these systems can
correctly interpret statistical assumptions.
This raises an important gap, namely that current evaluations tend to measure output
correctness, but not whether AI systems can detect incorrect assumptions provided by users.
This project is motivated by the question:
How can we assess trust in human–AI collaboration in statistical tasks via the agent’s ability
to recognize incorrect assumptions?

*Results to date:*
We have screened the relevant literature on missing value imputation with data agents
for MAR, MCAR and MNAR and collected existing datasets which we will use in our project
to measure the correctness of imputed values and assumptions.
The current dataset collection consists of 100 datasets from various domains, such as
health, business, social sciences and biology. The data sets were acquired from UCI using
the filters "202 <= Instances <= 15000" and "0 <= Features <= 50". Results were sorted by 
views descending, using the first 100 results.
